{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And Abraham built there the altar.</b> With the definite article. By way of the plain sense (peshat): the known altar, according to the commandment of the Torah, that its stones not be hewn. And likewise its known place on Mount Moriah." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash, <b>the altar:</b> it is not written 'an altar' here, but 'the altar' — it is the altar upon which the first man offered. It is the altar upon which Cain and Abel offered. It is the altar upon which Noah and his sons offered. It is the altar upon which the ancients offered." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah, the explanation of this Midrash is known: who it is that is called 'altar' — he is the one who receives first, and he is the end of the structure. And this is what it says, ״ויבן״ — 'and he built.' And assuredly it is the altar upon which they offered above and below. And in the portion of ויקרא I will allude to this, with the help of the Name." "<b>And he arranged the wood.</b> The known wood — those which he split in his house, concerning which it mentioned above, ״ויבקע עצי עולה.״ Or he called them by the definite article because not all woods are fit for a sacrifice." "<b>And he bound Isaac his son and placed him upon the altar.</b> They said in the Midrash: when he came to slaughter, Isaac said: My father, bind my hands and my feet, lest I see the knife and flinch, and the sacrifice be invalidated. I beg you, do not make a blemish in me." "<b>Above the wood.</b> R. Hanina said: this teaches that Abraham built the altar aligned opposite the Throne of Glory — it is written here, ״ממעל לעצים״ (above the wood), and it is written there (Isaiah 6:2): ״שרפים עומדים ממעל לו״ (seraphim standing above Him)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:11.785914531Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}